@flexiarg cascade-construction/own-the-noise-you-caused
@title Own the Noise You Caused — label your own garble before the agent spends effort on it
@status accepted 2026-11-11: proposed by 象 for 2 turns (Joe 2026-11-11: recurrence suffices); attested once used in a mission or a War Machine cascade
@provenance 象 proposals, ~/.emacs-graph/session-turn-analysis/<record>[.json].candidates.json; records: turn-WQZWl1 turn-XrAwwe; first proposed for: The operator's own previous message arrived broken (interrupted, truncated, mis-sent) and is now part of the agent's context.
@why [agency/loud-failure]

! conclusion: Explicitly mark the earlier message as noise, name the cause ('interrupted'), and let the real instruction follow cleanly.

  + IF:
    A prior operator turn was noise rather than signal.

  + HOWEVER:
    The agent cannot distinguish an interrupted transmission from a terse instruction, and may burn a turn interpreting or acting on the garble.

  + THEN:
    Explicitly mark the earlier message as noise, name the cause ('interrupted'), and let the real instruction follow cleanly.

  + BECAUSE:
    Same discipline from the operator side: a fault (broken transmission) is surfaced with its cause at the layer that produced it, instead of being left as a silent ambiguity in the record.
